Sleeping in a Loire Valley château means swapping the anonymous hotel room for a space with a history, grounds as your only neighbour and the silence of the Touraine. At the Château de la Huberdière, near Amboise, we have spent years welcoming travellers who are looking for exactly that: an exceptional night rather than a simple bed.

Here are seven ways to live that night, from a room full of character to the exclusive hire of an entire estate, and everything you need to know before you book.

## Where to sleep in a Loire Valley château

You can sleep in a Loire Valley château either in a room within a charming hotel or by hiring a whole estate for a group. The valley holds a density of châteaux found nowhere else in France, between Orléans and Angers, and the Amboise area is its heart: Chenonceau, Chaumont-sur-Loire and the royal château are all within half an hour.

The Huberdière stands in Nazelles-Négron, just across the Loire from Amboise. You are 2 hrs 20 from Paris, 30 minutes from Tours and its Saint-Pierre-des-Corps TGV station, and already far from the bustle. The estate is a 16th-century family home, with 10 rooms and suites looking onto walled grounds of fourteen hectares. The rooms and rates are set out on the [château stay](/en/boutique-hotel-amboise) page.

## 1. A night in a room with character

The first experience is to choose a room rather than a standardised room category. Our 10 rooms each carry a name and a decor of their own: Paradis Sauvage, Giboulée, Pêche, Bleu Indien. Old parquet floors, high ceilings, views over the centuries-old trees or the hillsides. No two are alike, and that is deliberate.

You are not booking a door number, you are choosing an atmosphere. To understand what one really looks for in a charming address in the Touraine, our guide on [bed and breakfast in Amboise](/en/blog/bed-and-breakfast-amboise) sets out what distinguishes a lived-in home from a place to pass through.

## 2. Waking up facing the grounds

The second experience happens at first light, windows open onto fourteen walled hectares where you hear the birds and not the road. The wooded park rings the château with its walls. You take your coffee on the terrace, walk barefoot in the grass, let the children run without watching the gate.

It is this calm that you come looking for by staying in a château rather than in a town centre. The night is truly silent here, and you wake to the rhythm of the Touraine countryside, not to car horns.

## 3. An evening at the host's table

Third experience, and no small one: dining on site without getting back in the car. Italian by birth, in the evening I cook a table from the market and the kitchen garden, and on Thursdays dinner is entirely Italian. On the other evenings, except Sunday, there are sharing boards, simpler, around a single table.

You dine together, the conversations linger, you leave with an address rather than an anonymous booking. This home cooking is as much a part of the stay as the room. The details of our [dining at the château](/en/chateau-dining-touraine) set out the formats and the evenings we serve.

## 4. A swim in the heated pool at sunset

The fourth experience belongs to the warm season: a heated pool, open from April to October, in the middle of the grounds. You swim late, as the light drops over the hillsides and the day of visits comes to a close. The pool sits apart, sheltered from view, reserved for the château's guests.

It is the kind of detail that turns a night into a stay. You come back from a day at Chenonceau, you drop your things, and you dive in before dinner rather than hunting for a terrace in town.

## 5. A night on exclusive hire, with friends or family

The fifth experience is to have the château to yourself. The Huberdière can be hired exclusively for a night or a weekend, with its 10 rooms, its 21 beds and its fourteen hectares reserved for your group alone. No other guests, no imposed schedule: the house is yours.

It is the format for family reunions, milestone birthdays and weekends with friends, with the kitchen at your disposal and the space to be truly together. We set out this format in our guide on [hiring a château with friends or family](/en/blog/rent-chateau-group-weekend), and the [large-group hire](/en/chateau-hire-large-group) page brings together the estate's capacities and options.

## 6. A weekend visiting the Loire châteaux

Sixth experience: making the château your base camp to explore. From the Huberdière, the finest monuments in the valley are within easy reach by car. You set off in the morning to visit, you come home in the evening to your grounds, without ever driving long or looking for a different hotel each night.

A few landmarks from the estate:

- the royal château of Amboise and the Clos Lucé, Leonardo da Vinci's last home, ten minutes away
- Chenonceau, the château over the Cher, twenty minutes away
- Chaumont-sur-Loire and its international garden festival, twenty-five minutes away
- the Vouvray and Montlouis cellars for tastings
- the Loire à Vélo cycle route, along the banks of the Loire

To build your itinerary, our guide on [visiting the Loire châteaux](/en/blog/visiting-loire-chateaux) gives the distances, opening times and visiting order that avoids the crowds.

## 7. An off-season getaway

The seventh experience is the one people often forget: coming outside the summer. Autumn colours the Vouvray vines at harvest time, winter has the charm of absolute calm and an open fire, spring wakes the gardens of Villandry and Chaumont with soft light and few people.

There is no bad season to sleep in a Loire Valley château, only different moods. An off-season weekend for two often gives the finest version of the estate: the empty paths, the sitting rooms to yourself, the silence.

## How much a night in a Loire Valley château costs

A night in a charming château usually ranges from 150 to 300 euros per room, home-made breakfast included. The price depends on the room chosen, the season and the length of stay. Rather than a single rate, we prefer to show the real availability and let you choose the room that suits you, on the [château stay](/en/boutique-hotel-amboise) page.

For a group, the logic changes: you think in terms of hiring the whole estate, per night, according to the number of beds used. At a comparable budget, a château offers what an ordinary hotel does not: the space of grounds, a home table and the feeling of being received rather than lodged.

## Bed and breakfast or château hotel: which to choose

The real choice is not between one label and another, but between two atmospheres that sometimes meet. You may look for the welcome of a bed and breakfast, that closeness to those who run the place, while still wanting the comfort and services of a hotel. That is exactly the spirit in which we welcome guests: a family home that has kept the welcome of a bed and breakfast, with the comfort of a charming hotel.

What you gain by sleeping in a château like ours is coherence: the room, the grounds, the table and the history of the place form a whole. You do not stack up a hotel, a restaurant and a visit, you live the estate.

For a night, a weekend or an exclusive hire among friends, write to us and we will prepare your stay. You receive a proposal within 24 hours, with the available rooms and seasonal advice.
